{
253: {skill_tested: "Identify an acoustic problem.", question: "Why is following the loudest beat unreliable in a procession?", option1: "Outdoor music contains no recurring rhythm.", option2: "Players cannot hear nearby performers.", option3: "Echoes and travel time can make an arriving sound late.", option4: "Concert halls amplify only footsteps.", correctAnswer: "Echoes and travel time can make an arriving sound late.", explanation: "Step 1: Distance and reflection separate arrival time from the current beat.<br><br>Answer: Echoes and travel time can make an arriving sound late.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
254: {skill_tested: "Identify the role of a figure.", question: "Recurring rhythmic figures function as:", option1: "signals for the audience to leave.", option2: "replacements for all rehearsal.", option3: "proof that the tempo never changes.", option4: "checkpoints that reveal position in the larger form.", correctAnswer: "checkpoints that reveal position in the larger form.", explanation: "Step 1: They let separated sections locate themselves and correct drift.<br><br>Answer: checkpoints that reveal position in the larger form.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
255: {skill_tested: "State central idea.", question: "Which statement best expresses the passage's main idea?", option1: "Moving ensembles sustain coordination by combining local cues, learned structure, and flexible correction.", option2: "One distant leader controls every beat directly.", option3: "Outdoor performance succeeds only when echoes disappear.", option4: "Written notation makes visual cues unnecessary.", correctAnswer: "Moving ensembles sustain coordination by combining local cues, learned structure, and flexible correction.", explanation: "Step 1: No single cue suffices across changing spatial conditions.<br><br>Answer: Moving ensembles sustain coordination by combining local cues, learned structure, and flexible correction.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
256: {skill_tested: "Explain local relay.", question: "Why do rear players align mainly with nearby musicians?", option1: "Rear players do not know the composition.", option2: "Nearby cues arrive with less delay than sound from the distant front.", option3: "Leaders are silent during every turn.", option4: "Local musicians always play different rhythms.", correctAnswer: "Nearby cues arrive with less delay than sound from the distant front.", explanation: "Step 1: Proximity reduces transmission delay.<br><br>Answer: Nearby cues arrive with less delay than sound from the distant front.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
257: {skill_tested: "Infer judgment.", question: "Not reacting equally to every discrepancy requires performers to:", option1: "ignore every change in neighboring movement.", option2: "increase tempo whenever an echo occurs.", option3: "distinguish useful evidence from harmless or misleading variation.", option4: "memorize the audience's clapping pattern.", correctAnswer: "distinguish useful evidence from harmless or misleading variation.", explanation: "Step 1: Some discrepancies reflect delay or stumbling rather than a genuine tempo shift.<br><br>Answer: distinguish useful evidence from harmless or misleading variation.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
258: {skill_tested: "Analyze rehearsal purpose.", question: "Why do rehearsals remove or disrupt familiar cues?", option1: "To reproduce one exact route permanently.", option2: "To eliminate recurring figures.", option3: "To teach audiences how to conduct.", option4: "To train musicians to recognize which remaining cues can guide them.", correctAnswer: "To train musicians to recognize which remaining cues can guide them.", explanation: "Step 1: Controlled disruption develops adaptable attention.<br><br>Answer: To train musicians to recognize which remaining cues can guide them.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
259: {skill_tested: "Interpret adaptation.", question: "Simplifying a transition near a crowd illustrates that:", option1: "flexible execution can preserve underlying structure under changed conditions.", option2: "ornament is the only stable part of music.", option3: "crowds always destroy the pulse.", option4: "a composition has no recognizable form.", correctAnswer: "flexible execution can preserve underlying structure under changed conditions.", explanation: "Step 1: The group sacrifices detail to retain coordination and form.<br><br>Answer: flexible execution can preserve underlying structure under changed conditions.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
260: {skill_tested: "Synthesize documentation methods.", question: "Why does the author say route maps and video belong in the study of procession music?", option1: "Written rhythms contain no musical information.", option2: "Spacing, gesture, and place are components of how the work is realized.", option3: "Interviews can replace observing performances.", option4: "Maps determine what notes composers must write.", correctAnswer: "Spacing, gesture, and place are components of how the work is realized.", explanation: "Step 1: The performance includes spatial coordination not captured by notes alone.<br><br>Answer: Spacing, gesture, and place are components of how the work is realized.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
261: {skill_tested: "Interpret the conclusion.", question: "The phrase many local acts of attention emphasizes that unity is:", option1: "an illusion caused only by buildings.", option2: "created before performers begin moving.", option3: "an emergent result of repeated nearby adjustments.", option4: "maintained through perfectly simultaneous signals.", correctAnswer: "an emergent result of repeated nearby adjustments.", explanation: "Step 1: Collective pulse arises from distributed comparisons and corrections.<br><br>Answer: an emergent result of repeated nearby adjustments.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
